7592186045417] [17592186068987 #edb/ref 1049 #edb/ref 17592186050089 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186068987 #edb/ref 1050 480.0 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186068987 #edb/ref 1053 #uuid "7b10485d-7fa4-5254-83ce-37fba428298f" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186069430 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186067750 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186069430 #edb/ref 1069 "q-53553" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186069430 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186069430 #edb/ref 1071 "![](images/ad/ad42b4bd62bf64e1ac086a60ace286b3e6d1c136f423d2ae6aabf6339cea9071.png)\n\nWhat length of fence is needed to border a circular plot of land with a diameter of $30\\,\\text{m}?$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186069430 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "21f64099-6f70-52f4-bd49-e0e0761a9208" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186069430 #edb/ref 1074 "The length of the fence needed to border the circular land is its circumference.\n\nThe formula for the circumference of a circle is $C=\\pi d,$ where $d=30\\,\\text{m}$ is the diameter.\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\nC=\\pi \\cdot 30=30\\pi \\,\\text{m}.\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186069547 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186069548 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186069547 #edb/ref 1061 10876 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186069547 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "3437ac3b-f6ef-5e78-8b46-bca7d485abd4" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186069547 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186069956 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186069548 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186069549 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186069548 #edb/ref 1061 10877 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186069548 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "51b49060-4f8e-51b6-a0fb-696a9394fe72" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186069548 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186068681 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186069549 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186069550 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186069549 #edb/ref 1061 10878 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186069549 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "0f70e49e-370a-5a2c-9320-28d0225c9e1e" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186069549 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186068682 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186069550 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186497477 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186069550 #edb/ref 1061 10879 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186069550 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "f9fe7dd8-677c-508f-836a-f00b1739d9cc" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186069550 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186068683 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186069551 #edb/ref 1061 10881 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186069551 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "fb4b1e1c-564b-5857-b040-cc788b2e5510" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186069551 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186068684 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186069956 #edb/ref 1064 "The **circumference** of a circle is the length of the path that goes around the circle.\n\n![](images/ac/ac8f31a99961c51cb53695d9daab017126d1316b720e6383b876824d7c7947bd.png)\n\nThe circumference is usually denoted by the letter $C.$\n\nOne important fact about the circumference is that, for *any* circle, no matter how small or large it is, the ratio of circumference $C$ to diameter $d$ is a fixed number! Specifically,\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{C}{d} = {\\color{blue}\\boldsymbol{\\pi}} \\approx 3.141\\,592\\,654...\n$$\n\n \n\nThis important irrational number is called **pi** (pronounced \"pie\") and we use the Greek letter $\\pi$ to express it. So, we can calculate the circumference by using the following formula:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}C=\\pi d.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nHere, our circle has diameter $4\\,\\textrm{cm},$ so its circumference is\n\n$$\nC = \\pi \\cdot 4 = 4\\pi \\approx 12.566\\,\\mathrm{cm},\n$$\n\nrounded to three decimal digits.\n\nSince the diameter is twice as long as the radius ($d=2r$), we can also write the formula for the circumference as\n\n$$\nC = \\pi d = 2\\pi r.\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186069956 #edb/ref 1065 2423 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186069956 #edb/ref 1066 #uuid "e75a0c81-7a99-51d8-85ff-de6951125d7b" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186069956 #edb/ref