ef 17592186497119 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186497291 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186497143 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186497291 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186497151 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186497291 #edb/ref 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045493 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186497291 #edb/ref 1077 "selection" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186497291 #edb/ref 1078 #edb/ref 17592186496803 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186497291 #edb/ref 1079 #uuid "99b743c8-67a7-5feb-bfaf-2c8a980270f9" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186497298 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186496767 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186497298 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186497015 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186497298 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186497056 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186497298 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186497120 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186497298 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186497146 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186497298 #edb/ref 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045493 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186497298 #edb/ref 1077 "selection" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186497298 #edb/ref 1078 #edb/ref 17592186496767 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186497298 #edb/ref 1079 #uuid "bc7cc937-333c-5443-91a2-63cd88fc55b0" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186497327 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186497275 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186497327 #edb/ref 1069 "q-53554" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186497327 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186497327 #edb/ref 1071 "![](images/43/43818f663454fe239d4b881a7cbd2f469391e80d87246a145f0fe1d294c9f71a.png)\n\nWhat length of chain will perfectly wrap around a circular tree trunk with a diameter of $2\\,\\text{yd}?$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186497327 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "0394adcb-093f-5edf-94a5-7360ea14bac2" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186497327 #edb/ref 1074 "The length of the chain needed to wrap around the tree is its circumference.\n\nThe formula for the circumference of a circle is $C=\\pi d,$ where $d=2\\,\\text{yd}$ is the diameter.\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\nC=\\pi \\cdot 2=2\\pi \\,\\text{yd}.\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186497332 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186497215 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186497332 #edb/ref 1069 "q-122016" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186497332 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186497332 #edb/ref 1071 "![](images/bf/bf0d8b99d9c681a6537aaeae828687719ac994766ce6d6322b50502e4fadef71.png)\n\nWhat is the circumference of a circle with a radius of $13\\,\\text{mm}?$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186497332 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "1630b08d-aa50-5fe7-9faa-2b7a697c8eb6" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186497332 #edb/ref 1074 "We use the formula $C=2\\pi r$ to get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}C & =2\\pi r \\\\  & =2\\pi \\cdot 13 \\\\  & =26\\pi \\,\\text{mm}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186497335 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186497291 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186497335 #edb/ref 1069 "q-75773" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186497335 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186497335 #edb/ref 1071 "![](images/73/73c890f1181c0073c0f56c31c8343872d5ad31e71b7a5755ea1633f52934a451.png)\n\nWhat is the circumference of a circle with a radius of $r=4?$ Round your answer to $3$ decimal places." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186497335 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "19bc1142-df4b-536a-85ea-0b9c7ee4c94e" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186497335 #edb/ref 1074 "We use the formula $C=2\\pi r$ to get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}C & =2\\pi r \\\\  & =2\\pi \\cdot 4 \\\\  & =8\\pi  \\\\  & ≈25.133,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nrounded to three decimal places." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186497338 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186497289 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186497338 #edb/ref 1069 "q-3735" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186497338 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186497338 #edb/ref 1071 "![](images/57/5782df9eb0a0055ef071647a133bf6cadf7c5121a160a4ffe2383efa899535e0.png)\n\nWhat is the diameter of a circle with a circumference of $12\\pi \\,\\text{ft}?$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186497338 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "1d3f49ce-877b-5ae1-8337-866d03e886d7" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186497338 #edb/ref 1074 "We use the formula for the circumference $C=\\pi d$ and solve for $d.$ So, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}C & =\\pi d \\\\ 12\\pi  & =\\pi d \\\\ d & =\\frac{12\\pi }{\\pi } \\\\ d & =12\\,\\text{ft}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186497366 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186497229 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186497366 #edb/ref 1069 "q-122023" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186497366 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186497366 #edb/ref 1071 "![](images/a8/a83fd5135d18ff095a601f6ff09658c1ce6ab877fe5004e62761dc93f530a624.png)\n\nWhat is the radius $r$ of a circle with a circumference of $7\\,\\text{m}?$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186497366 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "6a8defe9-ca28-57be-8b44-d4ab01b2ecd4" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186497366 #edb/ref 1074 "We use the formula for the circumference $C=2\\pi r$ and solve for $r.$ So, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}C & =2\\pi r \\\\ 7 & =2\\pi r \\\\ r & =\\frac{7}{2\\pi }\\,\\text{m}\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186497391 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186497298 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186497391 #edb/ref 1069 "q-122010" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186497391 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186497391 #edb/ref 1071 "![](images/92/92d75539c4e6e721349f78850a7c69e875d9c5415cbbad18592924d0ae66383e.png)\n\nWhat is the circumference of a circle with a diameter of $\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{\\pi }\\,\\text{yd}?$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186497391 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "b4c8a987-ad34-580a-aa0f-6a58f6c286c7" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186497391 #edb/ref 1074 "We use the formula $C=\\pi d$ and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}C & =\\pi d \\\\  & =\\pi \\cdot \\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{\\pi } \\\\  & =\\sqrt{2}\\,\\text{yd}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186497396 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186497219 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186497396 #edb/ref 1069 "q-75767" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186497396 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186497396 #edb/ref 1071 "![](images/c8/c88f19b18c77ae8473dbfb055518ab88558b03f2d20a169e548e034b67cb621e.png)\n\nWhat is the circumference of a circle with a diameter of $4\\,\\text{ft}?$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186497396 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "b9bd4bfd-1d1e-5b71-9461-1eac5c12f4bb" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186497396 #edb/ref 1074 "We use the formula $C=\\pi d$ and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}C & =\\pi d \\\\  & =\\pi \\cdot 4 \\\\  & =4\\pi \\,\\text{ft}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186497399 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186497261 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186497399 #edb/ref 1069 "q-122032" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186497399 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186497399 #edb/ref 1071 "![](images/e0/e046aafb09255a579c76a48f0f422627761a19451ae36a053424a8ac8a3332f7.png)\n\nWhat is the diameter of a circle with a circumference of $\\frac{5\\pi }{2}?$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186497399 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "c2fe80a7-5526-5b19-979f-2b18ba9fc425" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186497399 #edb/ref 1074 "We use the formula for the circumference $C=\\pi d$ and solve for $d.$ So, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}C & =\\pi d \\\\ \\frac{5\\pi }{2} & =\\pi d \\\\ d & =\\frac{5\\pi }{2\\pi } \\\\ d & =\\frac{5}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186497477 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186069551 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186497477 #edb/ref 1061 643569 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186497477 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "cc9d4179-5034-59aa-b0e6-47636130b8db" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186497477 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186497490 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186497490 #edb/ref 1064 "The problem of finding the length of a circle or a part of a circle often appears in real life. \n\nFor example, consider the pie in the shape of a circle divided into six equal slices, as shown in the figure below.\n\n![](images/48/48b416b9dffbf6e3e746e3fa8f90ec8043f937fe612e36e1219b5e9e8c368f22.png)\n\n How to find the total length of the border of one slice of pie?\n\nThe border of the slice of pie will consist of two parts:\n\n- the top and the bottom parts, which consist of two radii;\n\n- the right part (circular part), which consists of one-sixth of the circumference.\n\nFirst, let's find the length of the circular part. The formula for the circumference of a circle is ![](images/63/636374af24eb4d5c435de97b8e1ac460148deba4916b0954e3d1d2f7a364f9d7.svg) where ![](images/31/3122e2ea79ab0512edfce5c875a099de1a7067581b658d1b8fc9c105277aed36.svg) is the radius. Therefore, we have \n![](images/15/15bff81cb45d3df680e782e69534d2489388dfbcfd12b3cd579298d03f8bd452.svg)\n\nFinally, the length of the border is\n![](images/15/15037adbdea6b21d54991917b5dad6108c60257fd8f509300642343a101ebeeb.svg)" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186497490 #edb/ref 1065 15549 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186497490 #edb/ref 1066 #uuid "a14139c8-87eb-5e69-a268-c7cb088cf812" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186497490 #edb/ref 1067 "Calculating the Length of a Part of a Circle" #edb/ref 17592186045417]}
